  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2)/2, drawn at 0.5 × loss with contour lines. The x direction is steeper. Without noise, steps start to overshoot in x above a rate of 0.4 and grow without bound above 0.8.",
    "Step = −rate × (true slope + noise). The noise has spread 3·sqrt((64−B)/(63·B)): 3 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. It is centred on the true slope, so a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step, the way the ball will move. The grey arrow is the step the full-data slope would give. Both are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1).",
    "The run stops at 40 steps (finished), or as diverged once the loss passes 7, the loss at the drawn bowl's corners. A diverging ball and path are held at the bowl's edge, but the loss readout shows the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence indexed by run and step. Reset replays it from the start. 'New run, keep last path' moves on to fresh noise and keeps the last run as a ghost.",
    "When the ball is moving, the host's steps take five updates a second. Changing a setting mid-run affects only the steps that follow."
  ]
}